00:40:50.360 --> 00:40:56.300
When the podcast first aired, I was 22, unemployed after college, and living in a new, unfamiliar city.
当播客首次播出时，我 22 岁，大学毕业后失业，住在一个陌生的新城市。

00:40:56.300 --> 00:41:04.259
I was exhausted by the erosion of the industry I wanted to be in, the well-worn roadmap followed by so many of my idols no longer existed.
我对我想从事的行业的侵蚀感到精疲力竭，我的许多偶像所遵循的陈旧路线图已不复存在。

00:41:04.259 --> 00:41:10.199
Media was changing, and for the first time, I opted to, in the words of Ted Mosby, steer into the skid.
媒体正在发生变化，用特德·莫斯比的话来说，我第一次选择了滑行。

00:41:11.079 --> 00:41:18.032
I reached out to Craig on Instagram as a total Hail Mary, a shorter version of this message with my resume attached.
我在 Instagram 上以“万福玛丽”的形式联系了克雷格，这是这条消息的简短版本，并附上了我的简历。

00:41:18.032 --> 00:41:21.149
Never in all my life did I think I'd get a response.
我一生中从未想过会得到回应。

00:41:21.149 --> 00:41:24.746
Until one day, some guy named Alec emailed me and said, yep.
直到有一天，一个名叫亚历克的人给我发电子邮件说，是的。

00:41:24.746 --> 00:41:33.976
Since exactly one year ago, on April 14, 2025, I have been the show's digital content producer, which is a fancy way of saying, I do all our social media.
整整一年前，即 2025 年 4 月 14 日，我一直担任该节目的数字内容制作人，这是一种奇特的说法，我负责我们所有的社交媒体。

00:41:33.976 --> 00:41:39.191
I've been able to create a home for those who, like me, found themselves in this story.
我已经能够为那些像我一样在这个故事中找到自己的人创造一个家。

00:41:39.191 --> 00:41:46.024
Reading through the nearly 3,000 fan submissions we've gotten, I am constantly reminded that none of us are alone.
阅读我们收到的近 3,000 份粉丝提交的意见后，我不断提醒自己，我们都不是孤独的。

00:41:46.024 --> 00:41:50.520
It only took the right platform the right moment for us to find each other.
只有在合适的平台、合适的时机，我们才找到了彼此。

00:41:51.079 --> 00:41:53.624
In May 2025, I traveled from Washington, D.C.
2025 年 5 月，我从华盛顿特区出发。

00:41:53.624 --> 00:41:57.809
to Middletown, Connecticut, for Josh and Craig's alumni event at Wesleyan.
前往康涅狄格州米德尔敦参加乔什和克雷格在卫斯理学院的校友活动。

00:41:57.809 --> 00:42:07.930
After years of uncertainty and unnerve for the first time, I felt like I was exactly where I was supposed to be, chatting with Josh Radner about the best restaurants in Ann Arbor.
经过多年的不确定和不安，我第一次觉得自己完全在我应该去的地方，与乔什·拉德纳（Josh Radner）谈论安娜堡最好的餐厅。

00:42:07.930 --> 00:42:12.737
I'd dinner after with Craig, Alex, Alec, and Craig's mom, my best friend for the day.
之后我会和克雷格、亚历克斯、亚历克以及克雷格的妈妈（我今天最好的朋友）共进晚餐。

00:42:12.737 --> 00:42:15.055
Craig turned the conversation towards me.
克雷格把话题转向我。

00:42:15.055 --> 00:42:16.582
He asked, what inspired me?
他问，什么启发了我？

00:42:16.582 --> 00:42:18.335
What made me love storytelling?
是什么让我爱上讲故事？

00:42:18.335 --> 00:42:25.347
I don't remember what I said, because most of the answer was sitting across from me at a weird Italian restaurant in middle.
我不记得我说了什么，因为大多数答案都坐在我对面的一家奇怪的意大利餐厅中间。

00:42:25.347 --> 00:42:33.772
Six months later, Craig's debut novel was coming out from promoting the book I learned about Craig, his family, and their experience with disability.
六个月后，克雷格的处女作《我了解到克雷格、他的家人以及他们的残疾经历》的宣传活动即将问世。

00:42:33.772 --> 00:42:40.275
Growing up with an autistic brother, I can tell you the dynamics of that's not how it happened are accurate to a T.
我和一个患有自闭症的兄弟一起长大，我可以告诉你，事情并不是这样发生的，这对 T 来说是准确的。

00:42:40.275 --> 00:42:47.117
I brought my parents to the book's launch event, which I may or may not have traveled four hours on the Amtrak to attend.
我带着父母参加了这本书的发布会，我可能会也可能不会乘坐美国铁路公司四个小时来参加。

00:42:47.117 --> 00:42:50.284
My entire family has read the book and we can all agree.
我的全家人都读过这本书，我们都同意。

00:42:50.284 --> 00:42:54.695
It is the story we always wished had been written but couldn't find until now.
这是我们一直希望写出来的故事，但直到现在才找到。

00:42:54.695 --> 00:42:56.956
This show is literally everything to me.
这个节目对我来说简直就是一切。

00:42:56.956 --> 00:43:05.778
It has lifted me up in my darkest moments, made me think critically about the world, and now helped me create a career out of a pipe dream from high school.
它在我最黑暗的时刻鼓舞了我，让我批判性地思考世界，现在帮助我从高中的白日梦中开创了职业生涯。

00:43:05.778 --> 00:43:14.599
I am infinitely, incredibly grateful for you both, not only for giving me the opportunity of a lifetime, but for being the people I will forever look up to.
我对你们俩无限感激，不仅因为你们给了我一生难得的机会，而且因为你们成为我永远尊敬的人。

00:43:14.599 --> 00:43:20.650
I love and believe in this podcast, this show, this story, and I am so lucky to be even a small part of it.
我喜欢并相信这个播客、这个节目、这个故事，我很幸运能成为其中的一小部分。

00:43:20.650 --> 00:43:24.369
Oh, man, that was the best to go.
哦，伙计，那是最好的选择。

00:43:24.369 --> 00:43:26.170
Emily, you're making me cry.
艾米丽，你让我哭了。

00:43:26.170 --> 00:43:27.510
You're making me cry.
你让我哭泣。

00:43:27.510 --> 00:43:29.269
I'll call to tear dog.
我会打电话叫撕狗。

00:43:29.269 --> 00:43:30.670
And I also tiered up writing it.
我也分层写了它。

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:32.809
So it's OK.
所以没关系。

00:43:32.809 --> 00:43:34.710
What an honor to hear that, honestly.
老实说，很荣幸听到这个消息。

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:35.909
Like it's so beautifully written.
好像写得真漂亮。

00:43:35.909 --> 00:43:39.670
I really didn't know it was you for quite a while of that.
我真的有很长一段时间都不知道那是你。

00:43:39.670 --> 00:43:41.150
Some of it was stuff I didn't know.
其中一些是我不知道的东西。

00:43:41.150 --> 00:43:42.550
And I was like, wait a second.
我当时想，等一下。

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:44.389
This sounds like somebody I know.
这听起来像我认识的一个人。

00:43:44.389 --> 00:43:45.909
And it was great showbiz, Alec.
那是一个伟大的演艺圈，亚历克。

00:43:45.909 --> 00:43:48.869
You really did not give us any hint about what that was.
你确实没有给我们任何关于那是什么的提示。

00:43:48.869 --> 00:43:51.269
And then right at the perfect moment, Emily popped on.
然后就在完美的时刻，艾米丽突然出现了。

00:43:51.269 --> 00:43:55.309
It was a huge, great reveal, real editing and real life just happened.
这是一次巨大而伟大的揭露，真实的剪辑和真实的生活刚刚发生。

00:43:56.389 --> 00:43:59.710
And I'm, I'm, we're so honored to have you be part of it.
我、我、我们非常荣幸您能参与其中。

00:43:59.710 --> 00:44:02.590
And that the show meant that to you, you're such a good writer.
这个节目对你来说意味着你是一位优秀的作家。

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:05.150
It's so evocatively and beautifully written.
它写得如此令人回味和美丽。

00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:09.869
And yeah, thank you for being a fan and now working with us on this thing.
是的，感谢您成为我们的粉丝并现在与我们一起致力于这件事。

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:22.212
Yeah, Emily, I also, I also think it's, it's, it's, it, this letter should be wonderfully inspiring to people because, yeah, sometimes the thing that gets you where you need to be is just like your love of the thing.
是的，艾米丽，我也，我也认为，这封信应该对人们有极大的启发，因为，是的，有时候，让你到达你需要去的地方的东西就像你对这件事的热爱一样。

00:44:22.212 --> 00:44:24.043
Yeah, you know, and we felt that.
是的，你知道，我们也有这样的感觉。

00:44:24.043 --> 00:44:27.260
I don't know how we felt that over your Instagram message.
我不知道我们对你的 Instagram 消息有什么感受。

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:30.200
We just like, I just like, you Instagram messages us.
我们就是喜欢，我就是喜欢，你在 Instagram 上给我们发消息。

00:44:30.200 --> 00:44:32.475
I was like, she just seems pure of heart.
我当时想，她看起来心地纯洁。

00:44:32.475 --> 00:44:35.359
This just seems, we didn't know that much about you.
看来我们对你还不太了解。

00:44:35.359 --> 00:44:38.577
And you were interested in resume for such a young person.
您对这样一个年轻人的简历感兴趣。

00:44:38.577 --> 00:44:39.298
Yeah, you do.
是的，你知道。

00:44:39.298 --> 00:44:40.907
Yeah, you do, you do, you do.
是的，你这样做，你这样做，你这样做。

00:44:40.907 --> 00:44:46.509
And But there was an energy to it, and it's so clear that it was the right, it's just what Josh said.
但它有一种能量，而且很明显这是正确的，这正是乔什所说的。

00:44:46.510 --> 00:44:55.851
It's like you put that energy into the world, and we sensed it, and we knew it was authentic, and I'm so glad that we have you as part of the team.
就像您将这种能量投入到世界中一样，我们感觉到了它，我们知道它是真实的，我很高兴我们有您作为团队的一员。

00:44:55.851 --> 00:45:02.269
And it really was, please, anyone listening, make the reach out, say the thing, like take the chance.
确实，请任何倾听者伸出援手，说出来，就像抓住机会一样。

00:45:02.269 --> 00:45:03.949
But not, Joe, Emily already has the gig.
但不是，乔，艾米丽已经有了这份工作。

00:45:03.949 --> 00:45:05.150
You can't take Emily's job.
你不能抢走艾米丽的工作。

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:09.150
And you cannot take out a gig where we're filled up right now.
而且你不能在我们现在已经满员的情况下举办演出。

00:45:09.150 --> 00:45:15.590
But no, I think it's a great lesson in like, let your passions lead you to the thing.
但不，我认为这是一个很好的教训，让你的激情引导你去做这件事。

00:45:15.590 --> 00:45:18.909
And also don't be afraid to inquire, you know?
并且不要害怕询问，你知道吗？

00:45:18.909 --> 00:45:24.269
Like, we actually were understaffed and served and needed some help, you know what I mean?
就像，我们实际上人手不足，服务不足，需要一些帮助，你知道我的意思吗？

00:45:24.269 --> 00:45:26.190
Like you, so thank you.
和你一样，所以谢谢你。

00:45:26.190 --> 00:45:30.469
Yeah, take the shot, like shoot your shot and you did, and we could tell us from the heart.
是的，开枪吧，就像开枪一样，你就这么做了，我们可以发自内心地告诉我们。

00:45:30.469 --> 00:45:32.989
And it's been so great to work with you.
和你一起工作真是太好了。

00:45:32.989 --> 00:45:37.670
And again, like, you're the kind of person that we hoped was watching this thing we were making.
再说一次，我们希望你是那种正在观看我们正在制作的东西的人。

00:45:37.670 --> 00:45:39.710
And it turned out you were out there on the other side.
原来你就在另一边。

00:45:39.710 --> 00:45:41.230
We didn't know you yet.
我们还不认识你。

00:45:41.230 --> 00:45:44.349
But you made sure there would be a way that we'd get to know you.
但你确保我们可以通过某种方式了解你。

00:45:44.349 --> 00:45:45.469
And I'm so glad that we do.
我很高兴我们这么做了。

00:45:45.469 --> 00:45:51.150
And I just want to say thank you for those kinds of words about my book and you and your family have said such nice things to me about the book.
我只想对你们对我的书的评价表示感谢，你们和你们的家人对我对这本书说了很多好话。

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:55.150
I'm again, you're my target audience for that story too.
我再说一遍，你也是我这个故事的目标受众。

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:58.829
And it means the world to me that it connected with you guys truly.
它与你们的真正联系对我来说意味着整个世界。

00:45:58.829 --> 00:45:59.670
So thanks.
所以谢谢。

00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:01.309
Thank you for all of you both.
谢谢你们大家。

00:46:01.309 --> 00:46:08.630
Did you ever think about like, I'm not going to even bother reaching out, it's too much of a long shot.
你有没有想过，我什至都懒得去伸出援手，这太渺茫了。

00:46:08.630 --> 00:46:13.829
But like, when you reached out, did it feel like a bajillion, was it like Project Hail Mary?
但是，当你伸出手来时，感觉是否像万岁玛丽项目一样？

00:46:13.829 --> 00:46:14.829
Was it like, this is never going to happen?
是不是，这永远不会发生？

00:46:14.829 --> 00:46:17.150
But you just like, what made you do that?
但你只是想，是什么让你这么做的？

00:46:17.150 --> 00:46:19.710
Like, you just like, it worked.
就像，你只是喜欢，它起作用了。

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:20.710
You actually did it.
你确实做到了。

00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:21.710
You did the thing.
你做了那件事。

00:46:21.710 --> 00:46:25.190
But when you sent us that message, you must have thought this is going into the void, right?
但当您向我们发送该消息时，您一定认为这会消失，对吧？

00:46:25.190 --> 00:46:26.190
Yeah.
是的。

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:34.690
I mean, most things have like, I feel like I, everyone my age has been applying the like hundreds of jobs and they're all on LinkedIn and half of them are fake anyway.
我的意思是，大多数事情都是这样的，我觉得我这个年纪的每个人都在申请数百份工作，而且它们都在 LinkedIn 上，而且其中一半都是假的。

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:37.849
And I was like, I was like, that's really not working.
我当时想，这真的行不通。

00:46:38.210 --> 00:46:38.570
Yeah.
是的。

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:42.140
And one of my friends sent me the podcast and was like, Emily, you have to listen to this.
我的一位朋友给我发了播客，然后说，艾米丽，你必须听听这个。

00:46:42.140 --> 00:46:43.409
And that was before it came out.
那是在它问世之前。

00:46:43.690 --> 00:46:46.409
So I already had it in my head that like this was like my thing.
所以我心里已经有了这样的想法：这就是我的事。

00:46:46.409 --> 00:46:47.889
And I was so excited about it.
我对此感到非常兴奋。

00:46:48.250 --> 00:46:49.809
And what's what people had told me about it.
人们是如何告诉我这件事的。

00:46:49.809 --> 00:46:51.889
And I just felt like, why not?
我只是觉得，为什么不呢？

00:46:52.170 --> 00:47:00.969
I also remember thinking like I submitted a fan message and I'm really glad I also direct message you because now that I've access to the fan messages, I know that there's 3,000 of them.
我还记得我提交了一条粉丝留言，我真的很高兴我也直接向您发送消息，因为现在我可以访问粉丝留言，我知道其中有 3,000 条。

00:47:00.969 --> 00:47:03.610
So for how did I reach out to you directly?
那么我是如何直接联系到你的呢？

00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:06.289
Yeah, no, and we had other people reach out.
是的，不，我们还有其他人伸出援手。

00:47:06.289 --> 00:47:09.610
We had other people message us too, for whatever reason.
不管出于什么原因，我们也有其他人给我们发消息。

00:47:09.610 --> 00:47:12.449
We just felt like, I don't know, she seems like the right person.
我们只是觉得，我不知道，她似乎是合适的人选。

00:47:12.449 --> 00:47:17.469
Just like it was a weird little magical energy thing that when something's right, you kind of sense it.
就像这是一种奇怪的小魔法能量一样，当事情正确时，你会感觉到它。

00:47:17.469 --> 00:47:18.550
So it is cool.
所以这很酷。

00:47:18.550 --> 00:47:19.690
I think there's a cool lesson there.
我认为那里有一个很酷的教训。

00:47:19.690 --> 00:47:23.489
But I think it's so interesting to think about the show from like a Gen Z perspective.
但我认为从 Z 世代的角度来思考这个节目非常有趣。

00:47:23.489 --> 00:47:26.170
And I know Josh has talked at this, how it's very analog.
我知道乔希已经谈到过这个问题，这是非常相似的。

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:28.050
And I think it's just so timeless in that way.
我认为它是如此永恒。

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:38.429
It's been really interesting to see my generation who doesn't really have as much of a TV presence yet kind of latch onto the show, and it's so cool to watch from here.
看到我们这一代人并没有真正在电视上露面，却又有点迷恋这个节目，这真的很有趣，从这里观看真是太酷了。

00:47:38.429 --> 00:47:45.369
Yeah, and maybe liking the comfort of a time that was a little bit simpler, although it makes it so very old, I also kind of like it.
是的，也许喜欢简单一点的时光的舒适，虽然它让它变得很古老，但我也有点喜欢它。

00:47:45.369 --> 00:47:46.489
Emily, that was amazing.
艾米丽，那太棒了。

00:47:46.489 --> 00:47:47.329
Bravo.
太棒了。

00:47:47.329 --> 00:47:48.489
Thank you, Emily.
谢谢你，艾米丽。

00:47:48.489 --> 00:47:49.650
We're happy you're here.
我们很高兴你在这里。

00:47:49.650 --> 00:47:50.530
Yeah, we are.
是的，我们是。

00:47:50.530 --> 00:47:52.130
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:54.539
I am guilty.
我有罪。

00:47:54.539 --> 00:47:56.420
Please equip me.
请装备我。

00:47:56.420 --> 00:48:01.199
All sins are forgiven in New York City.
在纽约市，所有的罪孽都被宽恕。

00:48:01.199 --> 00:48:06.940
How we made your mother is hosted and executive produced by Josh Radner and Craig Thomas.
《我们如何造就你的母亲》由乔什·拉德纳和克雷格·托马斯主持并担任监制。

00:48:06.940 --> 00:48:11.579
And it's presented and distributed by the Office Ladies Network and Odyssey.
它由 Office Ladies Network 和 Odyssey 提供和分发。

00:48:11.579 --> 00:48:15.900
The show is also executive produced by Jenna Fisher and Angela Kinsey.
该剧还由珍娜·费舍尔和安吉拉·金赛担任执行制片人。

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:19.179
And the show is produced and edited by me, Alec Lev.
该节目由我 Alec Lev 制作和编辑。

00:48:19.179 --> 00:48:21.860
Our co-producer is Doug Matica.
我们的联合制片人是道格·马蒂卡。

00:48:21.860 --> 00:48:33.739
Our audio producer and mixer is Alex Reeves, at Point of Blue Studios, and our Gen Z Masters, our digital content producer, Emily Blumberg, and associate producer, Catherine Grove.
我们的音频制作人和混音师是 Point of Blue Studios 的 Alex Reeves，我们的 Z 世代大师、我们的数字内容制作人 Emily Blumberg 以及副制作人 Catherine Grove。

00:48:33.739 --> 00:48:35.820
Artwork by John Morrow.
约翰·莫罗的艺术作品。

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:41.460
Please follow, rate, and review the show on Apple Podcasts or your podcast player of choice.
请在 Apple 播客或您选择的播客播放器上关注、评分和评论该节目。

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.619
It really does help to spread the word.
它确实有助于传播信息。

00:48:43.619 --> 00:48:50.699
Our theme song is New York City by our own Josh Radner, with additional music by Craig Thomas and Andrew Majuski.
